when I think about the opioid crisis I  think what we're really talking about  our deaths due to opioid overdose and so  an overdose can occur really anytime  anyone uses an opioid and and when I say  opioid that's really an umbrella term  that includes things like painkillers  prescription painkillers so vicodin  percocet oxycodone all the way to heroin  and actually with the opium poppy which  is where originally these substances  were derived from so that's sort of the  big term and what we've seen in  Massachusetts and across the country has  been this dramatic rise and  unintentional death from overdose on  these drugs or medications and well  again this can happen anytime someone  uses an opioid most often when we see  these deaths it's due to untreated  opioid addiction so often we're really  talking about right now is a crisis of  opioid addiction and one of the reasons  that's so devastating and all of these  deaths are so devastating is that opioid  addiction is a completely treatable  illness and so really no one should be  dying from an overdose because we have  things we can do in the setting of an  overdose to save someone's life and to  keep people safe while they're actually  in the throes of addiction and we have  treatment that we know reduces the death  toll and helps people get into long-term  remission to lead meaningful lives that  really shouldn't be foreshortened by  this disease every day I encounter  people in recovery who inspire me and  who really give me hope around this you  know I think a lot of what we hear about  you know in the media is the you know  people who aren't doing well you know  people who are you know tragically dying  most of the people that that I have the  privilege to work with are are really  making great strides in their lives it's  an inspiring thing to watch and I feel  privileged to be a part of that  and it gives me it really gives me great  hope for the future the opioid crisis  began about 15 years ago when we saw an  increase in the prescription of opioid  analgesics or narcotic analgesics or  sometimes referred to as pain relievers  because we saw such an explosion of  prescriptions what we found was that  more people than ever before were  exposed to opioids in past generations  not very many people who are exposed to  opioids but with this surge in  prescribing with a genuine intention of  relieving pain those who have  vulnerability to becoming dependent on  opioids are developing an addiction  problem on opioids we're now exposed  with the prescription opioid pain  medication it seemed like there was an  abundance of these drugs everywhere you  know I had a lot of friends that also  had injuries that would be in prescribed  medication it just seemed like it was  easier for my generation as an underage  person to get access to a legal  prescription narcotics on the streets  and they get somebody to buy you alcohol  because of the accessibility of these  drugs the problem that we have currently  is that practitioners are literally all  over the map some people will not  prescribe anything at any for any  condition and some people will give 30  60 90 pills of of oxycodone or  hydrocodone and so I think what's  happening is that we're creating this  severe problem of expectations for  patients because they don't know what  they're going to get from which doctor  we need to do a better job with that I  pretty much started using drugs actually  I started drinking alcohol before the  age of 14 and then eventually from there  I progressed into snorting cocaine and  it was probably around the age of maybe  20 that I started snorting heroin the  opioid crisis is twofold actually it's  prescription medication and also powder  heroin and now the introduction of fact  No  in various forms of that so it's taken  on two folds where we have to attack it  from both ends  no period crisis it's affecting  everybody and it's in every community it  doesn't discriminate against anybody  doesn't matter poverty doesn't matter  race doesn't matter where you come from  every family is affected by it  [Music]  you
 

